Assume the hypotheses of minimal-deformation-over-T-empty. Set R := R^min and H := H_0(X, ω)_{m∅}, where H_0(X, ω) := H^0(X, (Ω^1_{X/O} ⊗ ω^{-1})_{K/O})^∨ = H^0(X, ω(−∞)_{K/O})^∨ (the Pontryagin dual, with the dual Hecke action of §3.2.4). H is an R-module via φ. Let q := dim_k H^1_∅(G_Q, ad^0 ρ̄(1)); q ≥ 1 by Proposition 3.3. For each integer N ≥ 1:
• Δ_N := (Z/p^N Z)^q and S_N := O[Δ_N], the group ring;
• R∞ := O⟦x_1, …, x_{q−1}⟧;
• Q_N is a set of primes with properties (1)–(4) of Proposition 3.3: #Q_N = q; x ≡ 1 mod p^N; ρ̄ is unramified at x and ρ̄(Frob_x) has distinct eigenvalues; H^1_{Q_N}(Q, ad^0 ρ̄(1)) = 0;
• φ̃_N: R∞ ↠ R_{Q_N} is a fixed surjection, which exists because R_{Q_N} is a quotient of a power series ring over O in q − 1 variables; φ_N: R∞ ↠ R^min is the composite of φ̃_N with the natural surjection R_{Q_N} ↠ R^min;
• Δ_{Q_N} := ∏_{x∈Q_N} (Z/x)^×, with a chosen surjection Δ_{Q_N} ↠ Δ_N and the corresponding Galois cover X_{Δ_N}(Q_N) → X_0(Q_N);
• for each x ∈ Q_N an eigenvalue α_x of ρ̄(Frob_x) is chosen;
• T_{Q_N} is the Hecke algebra T of §3.2.4 with Q = Q_N, and m ⊂ T_{Q_N} is the maximal ideal generated by m∅ and the U_x − α_x (x ∈ Q_N);
• H_N := H_0(X_{Δ_N}(Q_N), ω)_m.
The covering group makes H_N an O[Δ_N] = S_N-module. It is also an R∞-module via φ̃_N and the surjection R_{Q_N} ↠ T_{Q_N,m} given by Theorem 3.11. Hence H_N is an R∞ ⊗_O S_N-module.

For W ∈ {U, V, U_i(Q), V_i(Q)} (i = 0, 1) there is a smooth projective modular curve X_W over Spec(O). It is a moduli space of generalized elliptic curves with W-level structure, after adding auxiliary level structure at a prime q as in §3.3 if that is needed for representability (footnote 4). Notation:
• Y_W ⊂ X_W is the open subcurve of genuine elliptic curves and j: Y_W ↪ X_W the inclusion;
• π: E → X_W is the universal generalized elliptic curve, and ω := π_*ω_{E/X_W};
• ∞ is the reduced divisor supported on the cusps;
• for an O-module M and a sheaf of O-modules L on X_W, L_M := L ⊗_O M;
• for an O-algebra R, X_{W,R} := X_W ×_{Spec O} Spec R.

Let Q be as above, Δ a quotient of Δ_Q and n ∈ Z. By Lemma 3.28, Ω^1_{X_{U_Δ(Q)}/O} ⊗ L_σ^* = ω^2(−∞) ⊗ L_σ^* = ω^2 ⊗ L^sub_{σ^*}. So the §3.2.4 definition H_i(X, L) := H^i(X, (Ω^1 ⊗ L^*)_{K/O})^∨ gives H_i(X_{U_Δ(Q)}, ω^n ⊗ L_σ) = H^i(X_{U_Δ(Q)}, (ω^{2−n} ⊗ L^sub_{σ^*})_{K/O})^∨ (i = 0, 1). The left side gets the Pontryagin-dual Hecke action. Let Φ be the composite of isomorphisms H^1(X_{U_Δ(Q)}, (ω^{2−n} ⊗ L^sub_{σ^*})_{K/O}) →(D) H^0(X_{U_Δ(Q)}, Ω ⊗ ω^{n−2} ⊗ L_σ(∞))^∨ →(KS^∨) H^0(X_{U_Δ(Q)}, ω^n ⊗ L_σ)^∨. Here D is Verdier duality, KS is the Kodaira–Spencer isomorphism, and (L^sub_{σ^*})^* ≅ L_σ(∞) by Lemma 3.28. By the proof of [31, Prop. 7.3]:
• Φ∘T_x = x^{1−n}·T_x^{t,∨}∘Φ for primes x ∤ pNQ (printed 'for all x prime to NQ');
• Φ∘U_y = y^{1−n}·U_y^{t,∨}∘Φ for y | Q;
• Φ∘⟨a⟩ = ⟨a^{-1}⟩^∨∘Φ for a prime to NQ, because D exchanges ⟨a⟩^* and ⟨a⟩^∨_* = ⟨a^{-1}⟩^{*,∨}.
The transposed operators T_x^t and U_y^t are those of [31]. The paper displays (T_x^t f)(E, α_{NQ}) = Σ_φ φ^{t,*} f(E′, α′_{NQ}), the sum over x-isogenies φ: (E′, α′_{NQ}) → (E, α_{NQ}) with φ∘α′_{NQ} = α_{NQ}. To match the paper's normalisation of T_x and the conclusion below, this sum must carry a factor 1/x. Since φ^t: (E, α_{NQ}) → (E′, x∘α′_{NQ}) respects level structures, T_x^t = ⟨x^{-1}⟩T_x on H^0(X_{U_Δ(Q)}, ω^n), and likewise with coefficients in L_σ. The Pontryagin dual Ψ := Φ^∨: H^0(X_{U_Δ(Q)}, ω^n ⊗ L_σ) ⥲ H_1(X_{U_Δ(Q)}, ω^n ⊗ L_σ) satisfies Ψ∘(x^{1−n}⟨x^{-1}⟩T_x) = T_x∘Ψ, Ψ∘(y^{1−n}U_y^t) = U_y∘Ψ and Ψ∘⟨a^{-1}⟩ = ⟨a⟩∘Ψ.
